# Line endings are part of the determinism contract (spec §13.4), not a
# formatting preference.
#
# Git's default on Windows converts to CRLF on checkout. That alone broke the
# Windows CI leg — `rustfmt.toml` pins `newline_style = "Unix"`, so
# `cargo fmt --check` reported "Incorrect newline style" for a file nobody had
# touched. The deeper problem is the one after it: `borbax-render` emits SVG
# goldens compared byte-for-byte across a three-platform matrix, and a golden
# that differs only in line endings is a determinism failure that has nothing
# to do with the simulation. Those are the most expensive kind, because the
# first instinct is to distrust the physics.
#
# `eol=lf` makes the working tree LF everywhere, so every platform hashes,
# diffs and formats exactly the same bytes.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Excluded so git never rewrites them. None exist yet; the render goldens are
# SVG, which is text and must stay normalised above.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
